mic.oup.com\/biomet\/article-lookup\/doi\/10.1093\/biomet\/64.2.319"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[1977,8,1]]},"references-count":0,"journal-issue":{"issue":"2","published-print":{"date-parts":[[1977,8,1]]}},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1093\/biomet\/64.2.319","ISSN":["0006-3444","1464-3510"],"issn-type":[{"value":"0006-3444","type":"print"},{"value":"1464-3510","type":"electronic"}],"published":{"date-parts":[[1977,8,1]]}},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2022,8,3]],"date-time":"2022-08-03T04:41:17Z","timestamp":1659501677867},"reference-count":0,"publisher":"Oxford University Press","content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2017,9,21]]},"abstract":"<p>This chapter addresses the descriptive treatment of linear regression with a single predictor: straight-line fitting, interpretation of the regression line and standard deviation, the confusing phenomenon of \u201cregression to the mean,\u201d correlation, and conducting regressions on the computer. These concepts are illustrated with student discussions and activities. Many examples are of the sort commonly found in statistics textbooks, but the focus here is on how to work the examples into student-participation activities rather than simply examples to be read or shown on the blackboard. Topics include the following relationships: height and income, height and hand span, world population over time, and exam scores.<\/p>","DOI":"10.1093\/oso\/9780198785699.003.0005","type":"book","created":{"date-parts":[[2017,9,21]],"date-time":"2017-09-21T09:28:00Z","timestamp":1505986080000},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Linear regression and correlation"],"prefix":"10.1093","author":[{"given":"Andrew","family":"Gelman","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Deborah","family":"Nolan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"member":"286","container